# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of a deployment and applies
# the rules to matching paths. It is consumed by the host, never served.
#
# **Why it exists.** Without it, clicking Download showed the launcher as text
# in the browser instead of downloading it — `Throughline.bat` and
# `Throughline.command` are served with no content type and sniffed as text,
# and `throughline.sh` comes back as `text/x-sh`. Every download button on the
# page displayed a script rather than handing over a file, which is a dead
# download that looks like a working link.
#
# `_publish_launchers` copies this into `dist/` with the launchers themselves,
# so it travels with every release rather than being a step somebody has to
# remember on the day they upload.

# --- The three doors. These are downloads: force the browser to save them. ---
# `attachment` is what actually decides it; the octet-stream type is belt and
# braces for a browser that would otherwise sniff and render anyway.

/Throughline.command
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="Throughline.command"
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream

/Throughline.bat
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="Throughline.bat"
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream

/throughline.sh
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="throughline.sh"
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream

# --- install.sh is deliberately NOT a download. ---
# The page offers it as "Read install.sh first — trust, then pipe", which is the
# one honest thing a page suggesting `curl | sh` can do. Forcing it to save
# would break exactly that affordance, so it is pinned to plain text so a
# browser shows it rather than downloading or rendering it as markup.

/install.sh
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# install.py is fetched by the front doors rather than clicked, but a person who
# goes looking should be able to read it for the same reason.

/install.py
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Fetched by `irm ... | iex`, which wants the body as text.

/install.ps1
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Shown, not downloaded: it is instructions, and a file you must save and
# open before you can read it is one nobody reads.

/README.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
